WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.823 --> 00:00:04.539
Three words to keep apart: output, outcome and benefit.

2
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.411
Output is what the project delivers.

3
00:00:08.499 --> 00:00:12.655
The outcome is the change in performance that the output is meant to cause.

4
00:00:13.681 --> 00:00:18.371
And the benefit is the measurable gain for the organisation, usually in money.

5
00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:21.230
Projects deliver outputs.

6
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:27.341
The line, the managers who run daily operations, owns outcomes and benefits.

7
00:00:28.637 --> 00:00:29.554
An example.

8
00:00:30.243 --> 00:00:32.844
The output: the readiness check goes live.

9
00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:37.561
The outcome: cancellations drop from 14 to 8 percent.

10
00:00:38.591 --> 00:00:43.467
The benefit: about 1,200 fewer wasted crew-days a year.

11
00:00:44.509 --> 00:00:49.838
At 750 euros per crew-day, that is 900 thousand euros a year.

12
00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.610
The project delivers the check.

13
00:00:53.336 --> 00:00:56.041
But the line has to bring in the drop and the money.

14
00:00:57.365 --> 00:01:00.546
So name the benefit owner before the project starts.

15
00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:05.067
That is a line manager whose budget or KPI carries it.

16
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:08.390
So not the project lead, who leaves.

17
00:01:09.724 --> 00:01:13.462
Also agree the baseline before you start: the starting value,

18
00:01:13.648 --> 00:01:15.459
and exactly how it is measured.

19
00:01:16.471 --> 00:01:18.096
How do you count a cancellation?

20
00:01:18.797 --> 00:01:20.005
Over which period?

21
00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:21.963
Are storm weeks in or out?

22
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:23.861
Write it down,

23
00:01:24.105 --> 00:01:28.470
and get the controller to agree: the finance person who owns the numbers.

24
00:01:29.493 --> 00:01:34.102
Without a baseline, every result becomes an argument about definitions.

25
00:01:35.402 --> 00:01:39.385
Plan benefits tracking for 6 to 12 months after go-live:

26
00:01:39.512 --> 00:01:41.637
the day the change goes into use.

27
00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:44.917
The owner reviews the number every month,

28
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.878
and there is a check-in at 3 and at 6 months.

29
00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:49.945
And there is a trigger.

30
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:55.616
If the number slips past an agreed level two months in a row, the owner acts.

31
00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:00.044
Put this in the close-out plan, with names and dates.

32
00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:01.885
Or it will not happen.

33
00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:07.802
Be honest about attribution: how much of a change you can credit to your project.

34
00:02:08.835 --> 00:02:12.515
If cancellations dropped while a new contractor also started,

35
00:02:12.608 --> 00:02:13.804
you can't claim it all.

36
00:02:14.789 --> 00:02:19.944
Say instead: we estimate that 60 to 70 percent comes from the readiness check.

37
00:02:20.677 --> 00:02:21.583
And show why.

38
00:02:22.584 --> 00:02:26.601
The cleanest evidence is a comparison: a region without the check,

39
00:02:26.775 --> 00:02:28.017
over the same months.

40
00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:33.555
A board trusts a modest number it can defend more than a big one it can't.

41
00:02:34.912 --> 00:02:37.083
Boards are sceptical for good reason:

42
00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:41.274
large studies of projects find that benefits often fall short.

43
00:02:42.059 --> 00:02:44.927
The expert lesson of this topic looks at why.

44
00:02:45.913 --> 00:02:49.036
The module Land covered benefits for one project.

45
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.734
This topic takes the programme and portfolio view.

46
00:02:53.694 --> 00:02:58.024
This is how benefits leak: the project closes, the team moves on,

47
00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.765
and the old way creeps back within months.

48
00:03:01.745 --> 00:03:06.598
So ask the sharp question at every close-out: who looks at this number next month,

49
00:03:06.784 --> 00:03:08.700
and what do they do if it slips?

50
00:03:10.013 --> 00:03:10.942
An example.

51
00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:15.365
A changeover project at a manufacturer closed in March.

52
00:03:16.096 --> 00:03:19.695
Average changeover went from 90 to 45 minutes.

53
00:03:20.694 --> 00:03:24.339
The business case promised 300 thousand euros a year.

54
00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:30.033
In September the CFO, the chief financial officer, asks where that money is.

55
00:03:31.038 --> 00:03:32.466
You find two things.

56
00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:36.139
Changeovers have crept back to 70 minutes.

57
00:03:37.165 --> 00:03:41.147
And the freed capacity was never used: sales did not add volume,

58
00:03:41.356 --> 00:03:43.295
and the weekend shift kept running.

59
00:03:44.561 --> 00:03:46.001
Separate the two problems.

60
00:03:46.822 --> 00:03:47.983
The outcome slipped.

61
00:03:48.776 --> 00:03:50.854
That is for the operations manager,

62
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.604
who restores the standard with a short audit and a monthly review.

63
00:03:55.561 --> 00:03:58.812
And even at 45 minutes there was no benefit,

64
00:03:58.986 --> 00:04:03.421
because freed capacity is only money when someone turns it into money.

65
00:04:04.424 --> 00:04:08.708
Put the choice to the MT: cut the weekend shift, or sell more volume,

66
00:04:08.894 --> 00:04:09.823
each with a number.

67
00:04:10.845 --> 00:04:14.608
Then name one benefit owner, agree the measure with the controller,

68
00:04:14.816 --> 00:04:19.367
and book the 300 thousand euros only once that decision is made.

69
00:04:20.411 --> 00:04:25.102
And you tell the CFO this directly, without defending the old business case.
